\DOC LOCAL_SQR_P256K1_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SQR_P256K1_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-code goal past an inlined modular
squaring subroutine, replacing the current state variable by a successor state
and adding assumptions describing the squaring's result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} selecting which of the subroutine's
variants/entry alternatives to step through (two different values occur in the
observed traces). The second is a {string list} naming the result variable and
the input operand of the squaring: the first name becomes the new result value
introduced into the assumptions, and the pair together determines which stored
quantity gets characterised. The conclusion keeps the same {eventually} body,
but its state argument changes from the current state variable to a fresh
successor one.
{
   A ?- eventually ... s ...
   ========================================  LOCAL_SQR_P256K1_TAC n ["res"; "inp"]
   A' ?- eventually ... s' ...
}
\noindent Here the assumptions of {A} that mention the old state — the
program-counter value, the code-loaded fact, register contents and
memory-content equations — are discharged and re-added in {A'} in terms of the
new state, with an updated program-counter offset and an extended {MAYCHANGE}
frame relating the initial state to the new one, the previously accumulated
frame assumption being replaced by this extended one. {A'} also gains two new
assumptions: an equation defining the named result as the square of the named
input reduced modulo the field prime, and a memory-content equation saying the
destination buffer in the new state holds that result. Earlier definitional
equations of this kind are retained, possibly reordered, and side conditions
such as non-overlapping hypotheses are left untouched.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of forward-stepping
transformation, on goals of this one {eventually} shape.

\ENDDOC
